<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Troyes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augustobona, Trecae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Troyes is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in the Champagne region of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Founded under Augustus as Augustobona, the chief town of the Tricasses, it later took their name. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the capital of the counts of Champagne and the seat of two of the great Champagne fairs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a> wrote his Arthurian romances at their court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> was signed there in 1420.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/troyes.jpg" alt="Troyes" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KBWEi at German Wikipedia (Original text: KBWEi 21:50, 14. Okt. 2007 (CEST)) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troyes_centre_ville1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Troyes lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> about 140 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, within the Champagne wine region. Prehistoric evidence suggests that a settlement may have developed there as early as 600 BCE, and Celtic grave-mounds and artifacts have been found in and near the city. In the Roman era the town, called Augustobona Tricassium, stood at the meeting of many roads, above all the Via Agrippa, which led north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> and south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Troyes was the civitas of the Tricasses, whom Augustus separated from the Senones. Little of the early Gallo-Roman city survives apart from scattered remains and traces of an aqueduct, and by the late Empire the settlement had shrunk. Its later name came from Tricassium or Tricassae. The people had become Christian by the fourth century and the city became the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains">Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</a>, also called the Battle of Troyes, was fought nearby in 451, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Flavius Aetius</a> and the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric I</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>. In 878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a> received the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> from Pope John VIII at Troyes. After Norman raids at the end of the ninth century the counts of Champagne chose it as their capital, and it remained the capital of the Province of Champagne until the Revolution.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was an important international trading town, driven by the Champagne cloth fairs, and the troy weight for gold is named after it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashi">Rashi</a>, the commentator on the Bible and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>, was born there in 1040 and founded an influential school. In 1417 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, worked to have Troyes made the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> was signed there on 21 May 1420, betrothing Henry V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to Catherine, daughter of Charles VI, and making Henry rather than the Dauphin the heir to the French crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> and the Dauphin, later Charles VII, restored French control of the town in 1429.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Troyes is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Aube in the Grand Est region. Many half-timbered houses, mostly of the 16th century, survive in the old town, and it is designated a City of Art and History. Its Gothic churches include the cathedral of Saint-Pierre-et-Saint-Paul and the Basilica of Saint-Urbain, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Jacques Pantaléon</a>, who became Pope Urban IV in 1261.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Troyes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rashi of <b>Troyes</b>, in the 11th century, wrote the most widely used commentary on the Bible and Talmud; Moses Maimonides, in 12th-century Cairo, wrote the Mishneh Torah, a code of all Jewish law, and the Guide for the Perplexed, a work of philosophy; Joseph Karo’s Shulchan Aruch of…</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Kept to few occupations, many lent money at interest, which the Church forbade Christians to do; others traded, and the commentary of Rashi of <b>Troyes</b> (1040–1105) is printed in every edition of the Talmud.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>A church council at <b>Troyes</b>, led by a papal legate and inspired by Bernard of Clairvaux, approved a rule for the Knights Templar.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-13">13 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His grandson Odo II added <b>Troyes</b> and Meaux around 1022, encircling the Capetian royal domain and contending with Anjou for Touraine, which was lost in 1044.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-blois">County of Blois</a></li><li><blockquote>The counties of <b>Troyes</b> and Meaux were built up in the 10th century by the counts of Vermandois, descendants of Herbert II, and passed around 1022 to Odo II of Blois.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-champagne">County of Champagne</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome they formed a civitas with <b>Augustobona</b> as its chief town, later called Trecae after the people, the origin of Troyes.</blockquote><a href="/state/tricasses">Tricasses</a></li></ul></section>
